  <title>Capture interfaces</title>
  <p>A capture interface joins a switch of the lab to a network interface that this computer has already, as its Ethernet card: the packets of that interface go into the switch, and those of the switch go out of the interface. It runs <cmd>vde_pcapplug</cmd> as root, through <cmd>sudo</cmd>; see <link xref="problems#sudo"/>.</p>
  <figure>
    <title>The settings of a capture interface</title>
    <media type="image" mime="image/png" src="figures/capture-settings.png">
      <p>The settings of cap1: Plugged into, sw1; Interface, eth0.</p>
    </media>
  </figure>
  <section id="connection">
    <title>Connection</title>
    <terms>
      <item>
        <title><gui>Plugged into</gui></title>
        <p>The switch that gets the packets. In <gui>Nothing</gui> the capture interface is <gui>Not connected</gui>, and can't start.</p>
      </item>
    </terms>
  </section>
  <section id="capture">
    <title>Capture</title>
    <terms>
      <item>
        <title><gui>Interface</gui></title>
        <p>The interface of this computer, as <sys>eth0</sys>; <gui>None</gui> by default. The menu lists the interfaces of this computer when the settings open, but the loopback, <sys>lo</sys>. Without one the capture interface is <gui>Not configured</gui>, and can't start. An interface that this computer doesn't have, as in a project made on another computer, shows as <code>eth1, not on this host</code>, with a warning: choose one of this computer.</p>
      </item>
    </terms>
  </section>
  <section id="running">
    <title>While it runs</title>
    <p>The settings take effect when the capture interface starts again; <gui>Restart</gui>, under <gui>Process</gui> in its menu, does it at once.</p>
  </section>
</page>
